# Renderer Manager Integration

## Goal

Restore all current Horse renderer backends in GAME and make `Renderer::Manager` the only owner/lifecycle entry point for backend rendering.

GAME supports the complete current `IRenderer` set:

- Rasterizer
- Ray Tracer
- Path Tracer

Rasterizer remains the default renderer because it is registered first.

The Debug -> Renderer menu also exposes the shared rendering systems that run around the selected backend:

- Gaussian Splat
- Volumetrics
- Global Illumination

Post processing is explicitly outside this implementation. Horse keeps its current generic `PostProcess::Pipeline`, but GAME does not create passes or add post-processing controls in this work.

The Debug -> Renderer menu always lists all registered renderer backends. A backend that cannot initialize stays visible but disabled and marked unavailable.

This restores the earlier GAME architecture where the renderer set was registered through `Renderer::Manager`, adapted to the current GAME debug UI and current Horse renderer APIs.

## Rendering Architecture

GAME must not own a concrete renderer as its main renderer anymore.

Current:

    Renderer::Rasterizer renderer;
    renderer.init();
    renderer.resize(...);
    renderer.render(world);
    renderer.shutdown();

Target:

    Renderer::Manager renderers;
    Renderer::Rasterizer& rasterizer = renderers.add<Renderer::Rasterizer>("Rasterizer");
    Renderer::RayTracer& ray_tracer = renderers.add<Renderer::RayTracer>("Ray Tracer");
    Renderer::PathTracer& path_tracer = renderers.add<Renderer::PathTracer>("Path Tracer");

    renderers.initialize();
    renderers.resize(...);
    renderers.render(world);
    renderers.shutdown();

Manager owns every renderer instance, tracks availability, performs activate/deactivate transitions, and keeps the previous renderer active if switching to another renderer fails.

GAME may retain references returned by `Manager::add` only for initial/default backend settings. Runtime debug code resolves backend-specific interfaces through Manager rather than owning duplicate renderer state.

No second renderer abstraction, wrapper, registry, or GAME-side backend lifecycle state is introduced.

## Shared Frame Systems

Gaussian Splat, Volumetrics, and Global Illumination are not renderer backends and must not be registered in `Renderer::Manager`.

They remain shared systems in Horse's existing frame path:

    Global Illumination update
        -> active Rasterizer / Ray Tracer / Path Tracer
        -> Gaussian Splat
        -> Volumetrics
        -> existing PostProcess pipeline hook, if one is supplied
        -> display / composition / debug / UI / present

The same shared-system state is used regardless of the active backend.

Switching renderer backends never recreates, copies, or forks Gaussian Splat, Volumetrics, GI, lighting, environment, models, animations, or gameplay state.

## Backend Initialization

Renderer registration order is fixed:

1. Rasterizer
2. Ray Tracer
3. Path Tracer

All three registered renderers are configured `enabled = true` before `Manager::initialize()`. The enabled flag means the backend is allowed to render when selected; Manager remains the sole selector and calls render only on the active entry.

Rasterizer receives the current GAME defaults before `Manager::initialize()`:

- enabled = true
- viewport culling = true
- shadow resolution = `Debugging::Values::ShadowResolutionDefault`
- shadow cascades = `Debugging::Values::ShadowCascadesDefault`
- shadow distance = `Debugging::Values::ShadowDistanceDefault`
- shadow near plane = `Debugging::Values::ShadowNearDefault`
- clear color = transparent black

Ray Tracer restores the earlier GAME working default:

- enabled = true
- resolution divisor = 4

Path Tracer restores the earlier GAME working scheduling defaults:

- enabled = true
- resolution divisor = 2
- samples per frame = 2
- stationary phase grid = 2
- reset phase grid = 1
- moving phase grid = 4
- moving depth block = 2

These tracer values are required before init because current Horse tracer backends deliberately reject unconfigured zero-valued settings.

`Manager::initialize()` initializes every registered backend. Initialization failure for one backend does not fail GAME as long as at least one renderer is available. Because Rasterizer is first, it becomes the initial renderer whenever available.

If no renderer is available, GAME exits with a renderer-manager initialization error.

## Frame Lifecycle

All backend lifecycle calls in GAME go through `Renderer::Manager`:

- startup: `Manager::initialize`
- resize: `Manager::resize`
- frame rendering: `Manager::render`
- shutdown: `Manager::shutdown`

GAME no longer calls `init`, `resize`, `render`, `activate`, `deactivate`, or `shutdown` directly on a concrete backend.

Existing world state, ECS lighting, environment, model scenes, viewmodel motion, loadout, firing, UI, and camera code remain renderer-independent.

## Debugging Context

`Debugging::Context` changes from:

    Renderer::Rasterizer& renderer

to:

    Renderer::Manager& renderers

It also carries the GAME-owned Global Illumination entity so the Renderer menu can edit the shared `GlobalIlluminationComponent` without searching for or duplicating it.

Debugging code obtains backend-specific settings only through:

    renderers.find<Renderer::Rasterizer>()
    renderers.find<Renderer::RayTracer>()
    renderers.find<Renderer::PathTracer>()

The Manager remains the source of truth for which renderer is active and which renderer backends are available.

## Debug -> Renderer Menu

The menu structure is:

    Renderer
      Active Renderer
        Rasterizer
        Ray Tracer
        Path Tracer

      Rasterizer
        ...

      Ray Tracer
        ...

      Path Tracer
        ...

      Gaussian Splat
        ...

      Volumetrics
        ...

      Global Illumination
        ...

There is no Post Processing section in this implementation.

### Active Renderer

Always show one menu entry per Manager entry in registration order.

Example:

    Renderer
      Active Renderer
        Rasterizer        [selected]
        Ray Tracer
        Path Tracer       [Unavailable]

Available entries are selectable. Selecting one calls `Manager::activate(index)`.

Unavailable entries are disabled but remain visible. Their tooltip states that the renderer could not initialize on the current graphics backend/hardware.

If activation fails, the menu does not implement custom rollback. `Manager::activate` already attempts to reactivate the previous renderer and remains the authority for `activeIndex()`.

### Rasterizer Controls

Expose the existing Rasterizer controls:

- Viewport Culling
- Shadow Resolution
- Shadow Cascades
- Shadow Distance
- Shadow Near Plane
- Clear Color

These controls edit the Rasterizer object obtained from `Manager::find<Renderer::Rasterizer>()`.

They remain editable even when Rasterizer is not currently active, so settings are preserved and ready when switching back.

### Ray Tracer Controls

Expose only settings currently supported by Horse::RayTracer:

- Resolution Divisor

Range:

- resolution divisor: 1..8

### Path Tracer Controls

Expose only settings currently supported by Horse::PathTracer:

- Resolution Divisor
- Samples Per Frame
- Stationary Phase Grid
- Reset Phase Grid
- Moving Phase Grid
- Moving Depth Block

Initial values are the restored working GAME values listed under Backend Initialization.

Ranges:

