## Global Illumination Settings

Global Illumination has two existing layers of state:

1. per-world `Renderer::GlobalIlluminationComponent`
2. renderer-wide tuning currently stored privately inside `GlobalIllumination.cpp`

The debug menu exposes both layers without maintaining a GAME-side shadow copy.

### GI Component

GAME creates one dedicated GI entity at startup with a `GlobalIlluminationComponent` so the system can be enabled and configured from the Renderer menu.

It starts disabled so adding the debug surface does not silently change the current scene's rendering cost or appearance.

Initial component values:

- enabled = false
- intensity = 1.0
- bounces = 1
- photon mapping = false
- photon count = 4096
- photon radius = 1.0

Renderer -> Global Illumination exposes:

- Enabled
- Intensity
- Bounces
- Photon Mapping
- Photon Count
- Photon Radius

Component ranges:

- intensity: 0.0..10.0, step 0.05
- bounces: 1..8
- photon count: 1..1000000
- photon radius: 0.01..100.0, step 0.01

### GI Tuning

Refactor Horse's private `Tuning` into a public settings surface while keeping the existing setter functions working for compatibility:

    namespace Renderer::GlobalIllumination {

    struct Settings {
        std::size_t rays_per_probe = 64u;
        std::size_t probe_budget_per_frame = 4u;
        std::uint32_t minimum_probe_dimension = 4u;
        std::uint32_t maximum_probe_dimension = 12u;
        float bounds_margin_scale = 0.05f;
        float minimum_bounds_margin = 0.25f;
        float ray_epsilon = 0.001f;
        std::uint8_t maximum_bounces = 2u;
        std::uint32_t maximum_photon_count = 100000u;
        bool paused = false;
    };

    Settings& settings();
    const Settings& currentSettings();

    }

The existing setter functions become thin assignments into this same settings object. There is only one source of truth.

These defaults make the system valid/configured while the GI component remains disabled until explicitly enabled by the user.

Renderer -> Global Illumination also exposes:

- Paused
- Rays Per Probe
- Probe Budget / Frame
- Minimum Probe Dimension
- Maximum Probe Dimension
- Bounds Margin Scale
- Minimum Bounds Margin
- Ray Epsilon
- Maximum Bounces
- Maximum Photon Count

Tuning ranges:

- rays per probe: 1..2048
- probe budget / frame: 1..1024
- probe dimensions: 2..64
- bounds margin scale: 0.0..1.0, step 0.01
- minimum bounds margin: 0.0..100.0, step 0.05
- ray epsilon: 0.00001..0.1, step 0.00001
- maximum bounces: 1..8
- maximum photon count: 1..1000000

When minimum probe dimension is edited above maximum, maximum is raised to match. When maximum is edited below minimum, minimum is lowered to match. Invalid dimension pairs are never written to Horse.

Changing GI settings that affect generated data calls `Renderer::GlobalIllumination::reset()` so stale fields are not reused. Toggling only `Paused` does not reset GI.

## Renderer Independence

Renderer backends are presentation/rendering implementations only.

Shared systems remain shared:

- ECS world
- Transform hierarchy
- Camera
- Models / ModelScene
- Materials
- Lights
- Shadows where supported by the active backend
- Environment
- Global Illumination
- Gaussian Splat
- Volumetrics
- Animation
- Viewmodel motion
- Loadout/firing
- Debug scene inspection

GAME must not create renderer-specific copies of scene state when switching backends.

Switching renderers reuses the same `Ecs::World` and current gameplay/render-system state in place.

## Availability and Failure Behavior

Manager `Entry::available` is the only backend availability flag used by GAME.

Unavailable backend:

- stays listed in Debug -> Renderer
- cannot be selected
- does not abort GAME when another renderer is available

Activation failure:

- Manager attempts to restore the previous active renderer
- UI selection follows `Manager::activeIndex()` after the call
- no duplicated GAME rollback state

Resize applies to every initialized/available renderer through `Manager::resize()` so switching after a window resize uses correct dimensions immediately.

Shared systems remain configurable even if a specific backend is unavailable.

## Horse Boundary

Horse already contains the required backend abstraction:

- `IRenderer`
- `Renderer::Manager`
- Rasterizer
- RayTracer
- PathTracer

Horse also already owns the shared systems:

- Gaussian Splat
- Volumetrics
- Global Illumination
- generic PostProcess pipeline hook

Required Horse changes are deliberately small:

1. Gaussian Splat: promote existing hardcoded visual thresholds plus an enabled toggle into `GaussianSplat::Settings`.
2. Global Illumination: expose the existing private tuning state as `GlobalIllumination::Settings`, with existing setters preserved as wrappers.

No renderer architecture rewrite is required.

The current tracer implementations require valid non-zero configuration before init and require `enabled = true` to render. GAME provides that configuration before `Manager::initialize()`, matching the earlier working GAME Manager integration.

Post processing receives no implementation change in this work.

